    "case_id": "177",
    "case_summary": "An older woman with a history of diabetes and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation presented with sudden-onset chest pain. One hour later, the intensity of pain had increased.\n\nVital signs showed a normal heart rate and blood pressure initially. Later, her blood pressure fell to 60/40 mm Hg without arrhythmia or bleeding.\n\n- ECG findings:    At presentation: Normal sinus rhythm, up to 2.0-mm ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF, subtle ST-segment elevation in leads V7-V9, and 1.0-mm to 2.0-mm downward-sloping ST-segment depression in leads V2-V4. R/S greater than 1 in leads V1-V2.    One hour later: New ST-segment elevation in V5-6 and higher ST-segment elevation in inferior and posterior leads. No pathologic Q waves.  - Laboratory tests:    Initial high-sensitive troponin T level: 0.309 ng/mL (reference range, 0.00-0.03 ng/mL)  - Imaging studies:    1. Emergency angiography showed thrombotic occlusion of the distal left circumflex artery    2. Computer tomography angiography revealed contrast present in the myocardium and pericardial effusion\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute inferoposterolateral ST-elevation myocardial infarction\nB. Cardiac tamponade due to ventricular wall rupture\nC. Acute inferoposterior ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)\nD. Subacute left ventricular free wall rupture (LVFWR) complicating acute myocardial infarction\n",
